Universal Musical Intermediate Representation (UMIR)

Toward a Rigorous, Collision-Based Theory of Structural Preservation in Musical Representation

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Status: Research Specification V0.3
Supersedes: V0.2 (Research Specification)
Date: 2026

---

Abstract

V0.2 proposed a falsification test based on the condition that "at least one invariant is lost during compilation to existing standards." This condition is vacuous: it is satisfied by construction, because invariants are defined by the kernel and existing standards never declare invariants. Showing that a higher-level IR loses information relative to a lower-level target is not a discovery — it is a truism, analogous to observing that type information vanishes in assembly.

This document corrects the error. It reformulates the central question in terms of collisions under native equivalence, following the algebraic tradition of Lewin (1987), Mazzola (2002), and the representation-evaluation framework of Wiggins et al. (1993). The reformulated question is:

Does there exist a pair of kernel objects (k, k') such that every compilation into a target standard X yields X-equivalent outputs, yet practitioners judge k and k' to be different musical objects?

If such a pair exists, UMIR identifies a genuine expressiveness gap. If no such pair exists for any target, UMIR is redundant.

The document specifies:

· A formal setup with kernel objects \mathcal{K}, target standards X, compilers T: \mathcal{K} \to X, invariants \iota: \mathcal{K} \to V, and native equivalence \approx_X.
· Preservation as factorization: \iota is preserved by T iff T(k) \approx_X T(k') \Rightarrow \iota(k) = \iota(k').
· Loss as collision: a pair (k, k') with T(k) \approx_X T(k') but \iota(k) \neq \iota(k').
· A graded preservation measure \omega_I(\varepsilon) replacing the undefined \alpha of V0.2.
· A commutation test as a second, independent gap criterion.
· A thin-slice empirical protocol preceding the costly Phase-0 study.
· A latency-based use case providing concrete motivation.
· A two-paper split separating formal and empirical contributions.

The principle remains:

\boxed{\text{WHAT} \neq \text{HOW}}

but is now operationalized as a claim about which equivalences survive compilation, not as a claim about universal ontology.

It can fail. That is what makes it research.

---

1. Problem Definition

1.1 The error of V0.2

V0.2's central decision rule (§9.1) was:

If at least one invariant is lost for at least one piece, UMIR has an expressiveness gap.

This rule is circular. Invariants are defined by K; existing standards do not declare invariants. Therefore any invariant I \in \mathcal{I}(K) that is not explicitly representable in a target X will be "lost." The rule does not test whether X could preserve the structural content — it tests only whether X declares it.

The correct test must be sensitive to whether the target's native semantics can distinguish two objects that the kernel distinguishes.

1.2 The reformulated question

Let \mathcal{K} be the set of kernel objects, X a target standard, and T: \mathcal{K} \to X a compiler. Define:

· Native equivalence \approx_X: two X-objects are equivalent if every conformant renderer treats them identically. This is a semantic notion, not a syntactic one. It excludes custom metadata blobs that renderers ignore.
· Invariant \iota: \mathcal{K} \to V: a function from kernel objects to a value space V (e.g., cycle-period ratios, interval classes, grouping tree).

The reformulated question is:

\boxed{
\text{Does there exist } (k, k') \in \mathcal{K}^2 \text{ such that:}
}

T(k) \approx_X T(k') \quad \text{for every native-semantics compiler } T

\text{and} \quad \iota(k) \neq \iota(k')

\text{and practitioners judge } k \text{ and } k' \text{ to be different musical objects?}

If yes, UMIR identifies a genuine expressiveness gap: a structural distinction that the kernel captures, that practitioners recognize, and that the target standard cannot preserve.

If no such pair exists for any target, UMIR is redundant.

1.3 Why this is stronger

The collision formulation has three advantages:

1. It does not require a decoder. Preservation is defined by factorization, not by reconstructing k from T(k).
2. It is mechanically searchable. One can search for collisions by enumerating kernel pairs, compiling, and checking \approx_X.
3. It separates the formal condition from the empirical condition. Condition 1 is formal (checkable by a program). Condition 2 is empirical (checkable by practitioners). Neither alone suffices.

