2. Formal Setup

2.1 Objects and morphisms

Symbol Meaning Domain
\mathcal{K} Set of kernel objects Formal
X Target standard (MIDI 2.0, MusicXML, MEI, audio) Formal
T: \mathcal{K} \to X Compiler from kernel to target Formal
\approx_X Native equivalence on X-objects Semantic (renderer-defined)
\iota: \mathcal{K} \to V Invariant function Formal
d_V Distance on V Metric
d_X Distance on X-objects (for graded version) Metric

2.2 Preservation as factorization

Definition 1 (Preservation). An invariant \iota is preserved by a compiler T iff:

\boxed{
T(k) \approx_X T(k') \;\Rightarrow\; \iota(k) = \iota(k')
}

Equivalently, \iota factors through T:

\iota = \bar{\iota} \circ T

for some \bar{\iota}: X \to V.

Definition 2 (Loss / collision). A collision is a pair (k, k') such that:

T(k) \approx_X T(k') \quad \text{but} \quad \iota(k) \neq \iota(k')

A collision witnesses the failure of preservation.

2.3 Native equivalence must be semantic

The definition of \approx_X is critical. If \approx_X is defined syntactically (byte equality), preservation is trivial: any custom metadata that renderers ignore would count. If \approx_X is defined semantically (by renderer behavior), preservation is meaningful.

Operational definition. Two X-objects x, x' are \approx_X-equivalent iff, for every conformant renderer R in the target's ecosystem, R(x) and R(x') are perceptually indistinguishable (within a tolerance \varepsilon_r).

This definition aligns with the renderer semantics of the target standard, not with its storage format.

