3. Invariants as Symmetries

3.1 The algebraic approach

Following Lewin's Generalized Musical Intervals and Transformations (1987) and Mazzola's Topos of Music (2002), an invariant can be defined as equivalence under a transformation group.

Let G be a group acting on \mathcal{K}:

G \times \mathcal{K} \to \mathcal{K}, \quad (g, k) \mapsto g \cdot k

Definition 3 (Invariant as symmetry). An invariant is a function \iota: \mathcal{K} \to V that is constant on G-orbits:

\iota(g \cdot k) = \iota(k) \quad \forall g \in G, \; k \in \mathcal{K}

The value space V is the quotient \mathcal{K}/G.

3.2 Examples of transformation groups

Group G Action Invariant V
Transposition k \mapsto k + t (pitch) Interval classes
Tempo dilation k \mapsto \lambda k (time) Duration ratios
Cycle rotation k \mapsto \sigma(k) (cyclic shift) Cycle-period ratios
Retuning k \mapsto \phi(k) (frequency mapping) Scale-degree relations
Inversion k \mapsto -k (pitch) Interval classes (up to sign)

3.3 Collisions under symmetry

A collision (k, k') with \iota(k) \neq \iota(k') means that k and k' lie in different G-orbits, yet compile to X-equivalent outputs.

This is the formal meaning of "structural loss": the target standard cannot distinguish two kernel objects that are inequivalent under the symmetry group.

3.4 The role of practitioner judgment

Condition 2 of the falsifier — practitioners judge k and k' to be different — supplies the structural meaningfulness that V0.2 left undefined. The symmetry group G defines formal inequivalence; practitioners define musical inequivalence. Both are required.

