4. Graded Preservation

4.1 The problem with binary \alpha

V0.2 defined:

P_i(R, K) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{preserved} \\
\alpha_i & \text{approximated} \\
0 & \text{lost}
\end{cases}

with \alpha_i undefined and \text{Cap}(R) binary. This is inconsistent: the capability declaration is binary while the preservation measure is graded.

4.2 The graded definition

Let d_X be a distance on X-objects (e.g., edit distance on event streams). Define:

\omega_I(\varepsilon) =
\sup \left\{ d_V(\iota(k), \iota(k')) \;\middle|\; d_X(T(k), T(k')) \leq \varepsilon \right\}

This is the maximal invariant deviation among pairs whose compiled outputs are within \varepsilon.

Definition 4 (Graded preservation).

P_I(R, K) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } \omega_I(0) = 0 \\
\max\left(0, 1 - \dfrac{\omega_I(\varepsilon_r)}{\tau_I}\right) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

where:

· \varepsilon_r is the renderer tolerance (the largest d_X within which the renderer treats outputs as identical),
· \tau_I is the invariant tolerance (the largest d_V that practitioners in Phase 0 judge to be musically negligible for I).

4.3 Why this fixes \alpha

The graded definition:

· Uses \omega_I, which is computed from T and d_V, not stipulated.
· Separates \varepsilon_r (renderer-dependent) from \tau_I (practitioner-dependent).
· Reduces to binary preservation when \omega_I(0) = 0.

4.4 Capability vs. preservation

The renderer declares \text{Cap}(R) \in \{0, 1\}^n: which invariants it claims to preserve. The measured preservation P_I(R, K) verifies the claim. The gap between claim and measurement is itself informative.

