5. The Falsifier

5.1 Operational form

Falsifier condition. UMIR has independent value iff there exists a pair (k, k') \in \mathcal{K}^2 such that:

1. T(k) \approx_X T(k') for every native-semantics compilation into X, and
2. Practitioners judge k and k' to be different musical objects.

5.2 Why both conditions are necessary

 Condition 1 true Condition 1 false
Condition 2 true UMIR has value No gap (target distinguishes)
Condition 2 false Collision not meaningful No gap, no collision

Condition 1 alone is insufficient: two objects may compile identically because the kernel over-discriminates (e.g., distinguishing two objects that are musically the same). Condition 2 alone is insufficient: practitioners may distinguish objects that the target also distinguishes.

5.3 Candidate collisions

Three concrete candidates, in order of expected strength:

C1 — Cycle vs. truncated pattern. A 7-beat cycle that continues indefinitely versus the same events unrolled over a finite span. MIDI and MusicXML can encode the events identically, but cannot natively distinguish "repeats" from "stops." The invariant is the cycle-period ratio; the collision occurs when both compile to the same event stream.

C2 — Improvisation zone vs. one realization. A constraint set (e.g., "any pitch in the maqam, any rhythm within the cycle") versus a single admissible event stream. Both compile to the same events if the realization happens to be the one chosen. The invariant is the set of admissible realizations; the collision occurs when the target cannot represent the set, only an element.

C3 — Tuning as structure vs. pitch-bend. Two scales that differ structurally (e.g., just intonation vs. 12-TET approximation) but yield the same bend values over a given span. The invariant is the scale definition; the collision occurs when the target represents only the resulting frequencies, not the generating ratios.

5.4 The finite-horizon caveat

Collisions are easier to construct over a finite horizon. A pattern that repeats indefinitely and a pattern truncated after N cycles are distinguishable only if the horizon exceeds N. The protocol must fix the horizon explicitly and report it. Otherwise one discovers artifacts of truncation, not structural gaps.

