6. The Commutation Test

6.1 Definition

Let \tau_K be a transformation on the kernel side (e.g., retuning, tempo dilation with cycle phase preserved) and \tau_X the corresponding transformation on the target side.

Commutation condition.

\boxed{
T \circ \tau_K = \tau_X \circ T
}

The compiler commutes with the transformation if transforming before compiling equals compiling before transforming.

6.2 Failure as a second gap criterion

If \tau_X is not definable natively in X, the commutation fails. This is an independent gap criterion from collision: it tests whether the target's transformation algebra is as rich as the kernel's.

Example. Retuning a cycle-based kernel while preserving phase relations. If the target cannot represent "retune without breaking cycle phase," then \tau_X is undefined, and the commutation fails even if no collision is found.

6.3 Relation to the collision test

 Collision found No collision
Commutation fails Strong gap Transformation gap
Commutation holds Representation gap No gap

The two tests are complementary. Together they define a two-dimensional gap profile.

