7. Thin-Slice Empirical Protocol

7.1 Motivation

Phase 0 (cross-cultural study) is costly. Before investing in it, run a thin slice testing three candidate collisions against three targets.

7.2 The slice

Three kernel objects:

1. A 3-against-4 unaligned cycle (polyrhythmic).
2. A just-intonation scale (structural tuning).
3. A gamaka-like contour (continuous pitch trajectory constituting the identity, not ornamenting it).

Three targets:

1. MIDI 2.0 (UMP).
2. MusicXML.
3. MEI.

Procedure:

For each (kernel, target) pair, compile and check for collisions. Report:

· Whether T(k) \approx_X T(k').
· Which invariants collide.
· Whether the collision is an artifact of the finite horizon.

7.3 Precise expectations

What is not lost:

· MIDI timestamps already encode unaligned timing. The events of a 3-against-4 polyrhythm can be represented as a sequence of note-on/note-off events with arbitrary timestamps.

What is lost:

· The cycle structure — the fact that the pattern is generated by two cycles of period 3 and 4, rather than an unrolled event sequence. MIDI has no native construct for "this is a cycle."

What is partially lost:

· The gamaka may be approximated by pitch-bend sequences, but the structural identity (the contour is the note) is not preserved.

7.4 Output

The thin slice produces a preliminary gap report: which invariants collide, for which targets, under which horizon. If no collisions are found, Phase 0 is likely unnecessary. If collisions are found, they define the targets for Phase 0.

