9. Kernel Algebra

9.1 The five components

\boxed{K = \langle E, F, G, C, M \rangle}

Component Name Domain Examples
E Events Discrete objects Note, attack, silence
F Fields Continuous phenomena Glissando, drone, vibrato
G Graph Relations See §9.3
C Constraints Invariants Interval preservation, modal grammar
M Metadata / Modules Extensions Tuning systems, cultural grammars

9.2 The E/F split problem

The critique noted that for gamaka, the contour is the identity, not an ornament. This undermines a clean E/F separation.

Resolution. The E/F distinction is not primitive. It is a presentation choice: an event is a field with compact support; a field is a family of events with a limit. The kernel may represent a gamaka as a field (continuous contour) or as a dense sequence of events, depending on the module.

The distinction is retained because it is operationally useful (renderers may handle discrete and continuous data differently), but it is not ontologically primitive.

9.3 G: separate trees, cycles, and dependencies

V0.2's G bundled three different structures. V0.3 separates them:

Sub-component Structure Example
G_{\text{tree}} Hierarchical grouping Motive → phrase → section
G_{\text{cycle}} Cyclic structure Tala, ostinato, loop
G_{\text{dep}} Dependency "This voice enters when that one reaches phase 0"

Each is a distinct algebraic structure. Conflating them caused ambiguity in V0.2.

9.4 C and M overlap

V0.2's C (constraints) and M (metadata/modules) overlapped on grammar. V0.3 assigns:

· C: universal constraints expressible without cultural reference (e.g., "preserve interval ratios under transposition").
· M: culturally specific grammars referenced by name (e.g., "this is a raga in Bhairavi").

A constraint may reference a module, but the module is not itself a constraint.

9.5 Primitive admission criteria

A primitive p enters K iff:

p \in K \iff
\begin{cases}
p \text{ is required by multiple independent traditions} \\
p \text{ corresponds to an identifiable invariant} \\
p \text{ cannot be eliminated without measurable loss} \\
p \text{ has a formal semantics} \\
p \text{ is compilable to at least one existing target}
\end{cases}

The "multiple traditions" threshold is experimental, not ontological. It is revised based on Phase 0.

