10.2 Capability-aware compilation

Each compiler T_i: K \to \text{Target}_i must:

1. Preserve invariants the target can express.
2. Declare losses where the target cannot.
3. Refuse compilation when a critical invariant collides and no loss is acceptable.

The third responsibility is non-negotiable: UMIR does not silently degrade.

10.3 Latency use case

The strongest concrete motivation for structural representation is communication latency.

Earth–Mars delay is 3–22 minutes. Real-time event transmission is impossible. But structural representation — shipping cycles and phase instead of events — allows each node to stay phase-locked autonomously:

\text{Node sends: } \langle \text{cycle}, \text{period}, \text{phase} \rangle

\text{Receiver reconstructs locally: } \text{events} = \text{unroll}(\text{cycle}, \text{period}, \text{phase}, t)

This is not a theoretical advantage. It is an engineering requirement for any multiplanetary musical practice. MIDI 2.0, as a performance protocol, cannot provide it.

