11. Failure Conditions

UMIR must be considered failed if:

· FC1: The thin slice (§7) finds no collision for any (kernel, target) pair.
· FC2: Phase 0 finds no invariants expressible in K without modules, and no modules are needed either (i.e., the kernel is empty).
· FC3: The graded preservation measure P_I cannot be computed reliably.
· FC4: Practitioners consistently reject all collision candidates as musically meaningless.
· FC5: The commutation test fails for all transformations, for trivial reasons (e.g., no target supports any transformation).
· FC6: The kernel K proves formally inconsistent or trivial.

FC2 correction. V0.2's FC2 was that no common invariants exist across 3 traditions. This is not a failure condition: module-level invariants are the intended design.

