16. Conclusion

V0.2 was a step forward from V0.1 but contained a circular decision rule. V0.3 corrects this by replacing "loss of declared invariants" with collisions under native equivalence.

The central question is now:

\boxed{
\text{Does there exist } (k, k') \text{ such that }
T(k) \approx_X T(k') \text{ yet practitioners judge } k \neq k'?
}

This question:

· does not require a decoder,
· can be searched mechanically,
· separates formal and empirical conditions,
· and can fail.

The architecture — kernel algebra K = \langle E, F, G, C, M \rangle, graded preservation P_I, commutation test, thin-slice protocol, Phase 0 perturbation method, latency use case — is the machinery to answer it.

The machinery may be wrong. The kernel may be insufficient. The collisions may not exist.

It can fail. That is what makes it research.

