Appendix A — Formal Foundations

A.1 Basic Definitions

Definition A.1 (Kernel). A kernel object is a tuple:

k = \langle E, F, G_{\text{tree}}, G_{\text{cycle}}, G_{\text{dep}}, C, M \rangle

where each component is defined as follows.

Symbol Name Type Carrier
E Discrete events Finite set \{(t, p, d, a, \tau, \sigma) \}
F Continuous fields Finite set of functions \{f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}^n\}
G_{\text{tree}} Hierarchical grouping Rooted tree (V_{\text{tree}}, E_{\text{tree}}, \text{root})
G_{\text{cycle}} Cyclic structure Finite set of cycles \{(p, \phi, \sigma)\}
G_{\text{dep}} Dependencies Directed acyclic graph (V_{\text{dep}}, E_{\text{dep}})
C Universal constraints Finite set of predicates \{c : \mathcal{K} \to \{\top, \bot\}\}
M Metadata and modules Finite map \{\text{key} \to \text{value}\}

Definition A.2 (Event). An event e \in E is a tuple:

e = (t, p, d, a, \tau, \sigma)

with:

· t \in \mathbb{R}: onset time in kernel time units.
· p \in \mathcal{P}: pitch descriptor, where \mathcal{P} is a pitch space (see §A.3).
· d \in \mathbb{R}^+: duration in kernel time units.
· a \in \mathbb{R}: amplitude or dynamic level.
· \tau \in \mathcal{T}: timbral descriptor.
· \sigma \in \Sigma: articulation or gesture descriptor.

Definition A.3 (Field). A field f \in F is a function:

f : [t_0, t_1] \to \mathbb{R}^n

representing a continuous musical parameter (pitch contour, spectral envelope, spatial position). Fields may be defined over a compact interval or the whole kernel timeline.

Definition A.4 (Cycle). A cycle g \in G_{\text{cycle}} is a tuple:

g = (p, \phi, \sigma, \kappa)

where:

· p \in \mathbb{R}^+: period in kernel time units.
· \phi \in [0, p): phase offset.
· \sigma \in \Sigma_{\text{cycle}}: the cyclic content (a sub-kernel).
· \kappa \in \mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}: repetition count; \infty for indefinite cycles.

Definition A.5 (Dependency). A dependency d \in G_{\text{dep}} is a tuple:

d = (u, v, \rho)

where:

· u, v \in V_{\text{tree}} \cup V_{\text{cycle}}: source and target of the dependency.
· \rho: a relation among \{\text{enters\_when}, \text{exits\_when}, \text{synchronizes\_with}, \text{phase\_locks\_to}, \ldots\}.

Definition A.6 (Invariant). An invariant is a function:

\iota : \mathcal{K} \to V

where V is a value space. An invariant is admissible iff it is constant on orbits of a transformation group G acting on \mathcal{K} (see §A.4).

Definition A.7 (Native equivalence). Let X be a target standard with conformant renderer set \mathcal{R}_X. Two objects x, x' \in X are natively equivalent, denoted x \approx_X x', iff:

\forall R \in \mathcal{R}_X, \; d_{\text{perc}}(R(x), R(x')) \leq \varepsilon_r

where d_{\text{perc}} is a perceptual distance and \varepsilon_r is the renderer tolerance.

Definition A.8 (Compiler). A compiler is a function T : \mathcal{K} \to X that is native-semantics conformant: for every k \in \mathcal{K}, T(k) is a valid X-object whose semantic interpretation under \mathcal{R}_X is well-defined.

---

A.2 Preservation as Factorization

Theorem A.1 (Factorization criterion). An invariant \iota : \mathcal{K} \to V is preserved by a compiler T : \mathcal{K} \to X iff there exists a function \bar{\iota} : X \to V such that:

\iota = \bar{\iota} \circ T

Proof.

(⇒) Suppose \iota is preserved. Define \bar{\iota}(x) = \iota(k) for any k with T(k) = x. Well-definedness requires that if T(k) = T(k'), then \iota(k) = \iota(k'). By native equivalence T(k) = T(k') \Rightarrow T(k) \approx_X T(k'), preservation gives \iota(k) = \iota(k'). Hence \bar{\iota} is well-defined on \text{im}(T). Extend arbitrarily to X \setminus \text{im}(T). Then \iota = \bar{\iota} \circ T.

(⇐) Suppose \iota = \bar{\iota} \circ T. If T(k) \approx_X T(k'), then by the definition of \bar{\iota} being well-defined modulo \approx_X (which we may assume), \bar{\iota}(T(k)) = \bar{\iota}(T(k')). Hence \iota(k) = \iota(k'). ∎

Corollary A.1. Loss of \iota under T occurs iff there exists a pair (k, k') \in \mathcal{K}^2 with T(k) \approx_X T(k') but \iota(k) \neq \iota(k').

Proof. Direct from the contrapositive of Theorem A.1. ∎

Definition A.9 (Collision). A collision for (T, \iota) is a pair (k, k') with:

T(k) \approx_X T(k') \quad \text{and} \quad \iota(k) \neq \iota(k')

Definition A.10 (Collision set). For a compiler T and invariant \iota, the collision set is:

\text{Col}(T, \iota) = \{(k, k') \in \mathcal{K}^2 : T(k) \approx_X T(k') \land \iota(k) \neq \iota(k')\}

---

A.3 Pitch Space \mathcal{P}

The pitch space must accommodate both discrete and continuous pitch descriptors, and must permit ratio-based relations.

Definition A.11 (Pitch space). The pitch space is a disjoint union:

\mathcal{P} = \mathcal{P}_{\text{discrete}} \sqcup \mathcal{P}_{\text{continuous}} \sqcup \mathcal{P}_{\text{relational}}

· \mathcal{P}_{\text{discrete}}: finite or countable set of pitch classes (e.g., MIDI note numbers, named pitches).
· \mathcal{P}_{\text{continuous}}: real frequency f \in \mathbb{R}^+, or log-frequency.
· \mathcal{P}_{\text{relational}}: ratios r \in \mathbb{R}^+, interpreted relative to a reference.

Mapping between sub-spaces.

\rho : \mathcal{P}_{\text{discrete}} \to \mathcal{P}_{\text{continuous}}

assigns a reference frequency to each discrete pitch. The mapping is module-dependent (a Western module uses 12-TET; a maqam module uses non-tempered ratios).

Ratio-based invariants. For p_1, p_2 \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{continuous}} with reference p_0:

\text{interval}(p_1, p_2) = \log_2(p_2 / p_1)

Invariants under transposition are intervals, not absolute pitches.

---

A.4 Transformation Groups

Definition A.12 (Transformation group). A transformation group is a group G acting on \mathcal{K} by:

G \times \mathcal{K} \to \mathcal{K}, \quad (g, k) \mapsto g \cdot k

satisfying:

· e \cdot k = k for the identity e \in G.
· (g_1 g_2) \cdot k = g_1 \cdot (g_2 \cdot k).

Definition A.13 (Orbit). The orbit of k \in \mathcal{K} under G is:

G \cdot k = \{g \cdot k : g \in G\}

Definition A.14 (Quotient). The quotient \mathcal{K}/G is the set of orbits. Any function \iota : \mathcal{K} \to V constant on orbits factors through \mathcal{K}/G.

Standard transformation groups:

Group Generator Action on k Orbit dimension
\mathbb{Z} (transposition) +1 semitone p_i \mapsto p_i + 1 Pitch classes
\mathbb{R}^+ (tempo dilation) \lambda t_i \mapsto \lambda t_i Duration ratios
\mathbb{Z}_p (cycle rotation) \sigma phase \phi \mapsto \phi + 1 \mod p Cycle classes
O(2) (inversion) -1 p_i \mapsto -p_i Interval classes
\text{Aff}^+(\mathbb{R}) (retuning) \phi f_i \mapsto \phi(f_i) Scale-degree relations

Composition. Groups can be composed:

G_{\text{total}} = G_{\text{pitch}} \times G_{\text{time}} \times G_{\text{cycle}} \times \cdots

An invariant under the product group is simultaneously invariant under each factor.

---

A.5 Graded Preservation

Definition A.15 (Deviation functional). For invariant \iota : \mathcal{K} \to V with metric d_V, and compiler T : \mathcal{K} \to X with metric d_X:

\omega_\iota(\varepsilon) = \sup \left\{ d_V(\iota(k), \iota(k')) : d_X(T(k), T(k')) \leq \varepsilon \right\}

Theorem A.2 (Monotonicity). \omega_\iota is non-decreasing in \varepsilon.

Proof. If \varepsilon_1 \leq \varepsilon_2, then \{d_X \leq \varepsilon_1\} \subseteq \{d_X \leq \varepsilon_2\}, so the supremum over the smaller set is at most the supremum over the larger set. ∎

Definition A.16 (Graded preservation). Given:

· renderer tolerance \varepsilon_r \geq 0,
· invariant tolerance \tau_\iota > 0,

the graded preservation of \iota under T with respect to X is:

P_\iota(T, X) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } \omega_\iota(0) = 0 \\[4pt]
\max\left(0, \; 1 - \dfrac{\omega_\iota(\varepsilon_r)}{\tau_\iota}\right) & \text{if } \omega_\iota(0) > 0
\end{cases}

Properties.

· P_\iota = 1 iff no collision occurs at \varepsilon = 0.
· P_\iota = 0 iff \omega_\iota(\varepsilon_r) \geq \tau_\iota.
· P_\iota \in (0, 1) for partial preservation.

Theorem A.3 (Binary-binary consistency). If \tau_\iota = \varepsilon_r = 0, then P_\iota \in \{0, 1\} and:

P_\iota = 1 \iff \text{Col}(T, \iota) = \varnothing

Proof. If \omega_\iota(0) = 0, then no collision exists at \varepsilon = 0. If \omega_\iota(0) > 0, then by definition there exists (k, k') with d_X(T(k), T(k')) = 0 (i.e., T(k) = T(k') syntactically, hence T(k) \approx_X T(k') under any reasonable semantics) and d_V(\iota(k), \iota(k')) > 0, so \iota(k) \neq \iota(k'). Hence Col ≠ ∅. The formula gives P_\iota = \max(0, 1 - \infty / 0), which we interpret as 0. ∎

---

A.6 Commutation

Definition A.17 (Transformation action on target). A transformation \tau_X acts on X if there is a well-defined function \tau_X : X \to X such that \tau_X is native-semantics conformant.

Definition A.18 (Commutation). A compiler T commutes with a pair (\tau_\mathcal{K}, \tau_X) iff:

T \circ \tau_\mathcal{K} = \tau_X \circ T

as functions \mathcal{K} \to X.

Theorem A.4 (Commutation failure criterion). If \tau_X is not definable natively in X, then no compiler T can satisfy commutation for \tau_\mathcal{K} nontrivial.

Proof. If \tau_X is undefined, the equation T \circ \tau_\mathcal{K} = \tau_X \circ T has no right-hand side. ∎

Corollary A.2. Commutation failure is an independent gap criterion from collision.

Proof. A collision requires a pair (k, k') with T(k) \approx_X T(k') but \iota(k) \neq \iota(k'). Commutation failure requires a transformation \tau_\mathcal{K} whose target counterpart \tau_X does not exist. The two conditions are logically independent: a target may distinguish all kernel pairs (no collisions) yet not support a transformation; conversely, a target may support all transformations yet collapse two kernel pairs. ∎

