Appendix B — Worked Collision Examples

B.1 Example: Cycle vs. Truncated Pattern

B.1.1 Kernel objects

Let k_1 be a 7-beat cycle repeating indefinitely:

k_1 = \langle E_1, \varnothing, \varnothing, g, \varnothing, \varnothing, \varnothing \rangle

where g = (7, 0, \sigma_7, \infty) and \sigma_7 contains events at t = 0, 2, 4.

Let k_2 be the same events unrolled over a finite span, say 21 beats (3 cycles):

k_2 = \langle E_2, \varnothing, \varnothing, \varnothing, \varnothing, \varnothing, \varnothing \rangle

where E_2 = \{(0, p_0, 0.5, a_0, \tau_0, \sigma_0), (2, p_0, 0.5, a_0, \tau_0, \sigma_0), (4, p_0, 0.5, a_0, \tau_0, \sigma_0), \ldots\} with 9 events over [0, 21).

B.1.2 Invariant

\iota_{\text{cycle}}(k) = \begin{cases} p & \text{if } k \text{ contains a cycle of period } p \\ \bot & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

\iota_{\text{cycle}}(k_1) = 7; \iota_{\text{cycle}}(k_2) = \bot.

B.1.3 Compilation to MIDI 2.0

A native-semantics MIDI 2.0 compiler T_{\text{MIDI}} produces a UMP stream. The stream encodes note-on/note-off events at the specified timestamps. Both k_1 and k_2 compile to the same event stream over the horizon [0, 21):

T_{\text{MIDI}}(k_1) \approx_{\text{MIDI}} T_{\text{MIDI}}(k_2)

because the renderer plays the same events over the horizon.

B.1.4 Collision

\iota_{\text{cycle}}(k_1) = 7 \neq \bot = \iota_{\text{cycle}}(k_2)

Hence (k_1, k_2) \in \text{Col}(T_{\text{MIDI}}, \iota_{\text{cycle}}).

B.1.5 Finite horizon caveat

If the horizon is extended indefinitely, k_2 must be extended as well, and its events must be enumerated infinitely. In practice, a finite horizon H is fixed. For H \geq 21, the collision is real: k_1 and k_2 are indistinguishable over [0, H) but differ in cycle structure.

B.1.6 Practitioner judgment

A practitioner of a cycle-based tradition (e.g., tala, gamelan) judges k_1 and k_2 as different musical objects: k_1 is a cycle, k_2 is a finite pattern. The collision is therefore structurally meaningful.

---

B.2 Example: Improvisation Zone vs. Realization

B.2.1 Kernel objects

Let k_1 be an improvisation zone:

k_1 = \langle \varnothing, \varnothing, \varnothing, g, \varnothing, C_{\text{improv}}, M_{\text{maqam}} \rangle

where C_{\text{improv}} declares: "any pitch in maqam Rast, any rhythm aligned to cycle g, duration between 0.5 and 2 beats."

Let k_2 be one admissible realization: a specific event sequence satisfying C_{\text{improv}}.

B.2.2 Invariant

\iota_{\text{set}}(k) = \{\text{admissible event streams consistent with } k\}

\iota_{\text{set}}(k_1) is an infinite set; \iota_{\text{set}}(k_2) is a singleton.

B.2.3 Compilation to MusicXML

A native-semantics MusicXML compiler produces a note-event document. If T(k_1) resolves the improvisation zone by choosing the realization k_2, then:

T_{\text{MXML}}(k_1) \approx_{\text{MXML}} T_{\text{MXML}}(k_2)

Both are the same notation document.

B.2.4 Collision

\iota_{\text{set}}(k_1) \neq \iota_{\text{set}}(k_2)

Hence (k_1, k_2) \in \text{Col}(T_{\text{MXML}}, \iota_{\text{set}}).

B.2.5 Practitioner judgment

A practitioner of an improvisational tradition (jazz, maqam, flamenco) judges k_1 and k_2 as different: k_1 invites performance; k_2 documents a performance. The collision is structurally meaningful.

---

B.3 Example: Structural Tuning vs. Pitch-Bend

B.3.1 Kernel objects

Let k_1 encode a just-intonation scale as a module reference:

M_{\text{tuning}}(k_1) = \text{JI\_5limit}

with pitches \{1, 9/8, 5/4, 4/3, 3/2, 5/3, 15/8\}.

Let k_2 encode the same pitches as 12-TET with pitch-bend offsets:

E_2 = \{(0, \text{C}, \ldots), (0, \text{D} - 4\text{c}, \ldots), (0, \text{E} - 14\text{c}, \ldots), \ldots\}

B.3.2 Invariant

\iota_{\text{scale}}(k) = \{\text{generating ratios of the scale}\}

\iota_{\text{scale}}(k_1) = \{9/8, 10/9, 16/15, \ldots\} (JI ratios); \iota_{\text{scale}}(k_2) = \{\text{bend values}\} (approximations).

B.3.3 Compilation to MIDI 2.0

Both compile to the same pitch-bend sequence over the given span:

T_{\text{MIDI}}(k_1) \approx_{\text{MIDI}} T_{\text{MIDI}}(k_2)

B.3.4 Collision

\iota_{\text{scale}}(k_1) \neq \iota_{\text{scale}}(k_2)

The collision is real, but subject to rounding tolerance: if \varepsilon_r is large enough, the two bend sequences may be perceptually indistinguishable, in which case the collision is not meaningful. This is why \tau_\iota must be set by practitioners: a JI practitioner will reject the 12-TET approximation; a casual listener may not.

