Appendix D — Graded Preservation Derivation

D.1 Derivation from First Principles

Consider a kernel k and its compilation T(k). A renderer R produces a physical realization R(T(k)). Define the structural content of the realization as:

\iota_R(k) = \iota_{\text{measured}}(R(T(k)))

where \iota_{\text{measured}} is an analyzer that extracts invariant values from the realization.

The deviation for k is:

\delta(k) = d_V(\iota(k), \iota_R(k))

The graded preservation aggregates \delta over \mathcal{K}:

P_\iota = 1 - \frac{\mathbb{E}_{k \sim \mu}[\delta(k)]}{\tau_\iota}

where \mu is a distribution over kernel objects.

Comparison with Definition A.16. The supremum-based definition is more conservative; the expectation-based version is more aligned with practice. The choice depends on whether the application prioritizes worst-case or average-case preservation.

D.2 Choosing \varepsilon_r

The renderer tolerance \varepsilon_r is determined by:

1. Perceptual limits. Below a just-noticeable difference (JND), outputs are perceptually identical.
2. Renderer specifications. Digital-to-analog converters, speaker responses, and room acoustics introduce noise floors.
3. Analysis windowing. Short windows have lower frequency resolution; long windows have lower temporal resolution.

Recommendation. Set \varepsilon_r by calibrating a known-equivalent pair (k, k) through multiple renderers and measuring d_X at the output. The 95th percentile of these measurements gives \varepsilon_r.

D.3 Choosing \tau_\iota

The invariant tolerance \tau_\iota is determined by Phase 0:

1. Practitioner judgments. Present pairs (k, k') with d_V(\iota(k), \iota(k')) = \delta and ask if they are the same musical object.
2. Threshold estimation. Find the largest \delta for which practitioners consistently say "same."
3. Aggregation. For multiple practitioners, use median or a majority rule.

Recommendation. Report \tau_\iota with confidence intervals. Different traditions may have different \tau_\iota; the module system accommodates this.

