Appendix G — Phase 0 Method Details

G.1 Interview Protocol

G.1.1 Participants

For each tradition, recruit 3–5 practitioners with:

· At least 10 years of practice.
· Recognition by peers as knowledgeable.
· Willingness to engage with unfamiliar representations.

G.1.2 Base Objects

Select 3–5 short musical objects from the tradition, chosen to exercise:

· Tuning (non-tempered intervals where applicable).
· Rhythm (cycles, meters, polyrhythms).
· Ornamentation (gamaka, tarkib, vibrato).
· Structure (hierarchy, repetition, variation).
· Improvisation (if applicable).

G.1.3 Procedure

1. Presentation. Show the practitioner a representation of the base object (native notation, audio recording, or performance).
2. Free description. Ask the practitioner to describe what makes the object "this piece."
3. Perturbation tests. Apply perturbations in sequence:
   · Transpose by a small interval.
   · Change tempo by 10%.
   · Shift cycle phase by 25%.
   · Replace an ornament with a different one.
   · Extend a cycle by one repetition.
4. Identity judgment. After each perturbation, ask: Is this still the same piece?
5. Elaboration. Ask why.

G.2 Perturbation Categories

Category Perturbation Invariant tested
Pitch Transpose ±1 semitone Interval preservation
Pitch Retune within scale Scale-degree preservation
Time Tempo ×1.1 Duration-ratio preservation
Time Cycle phase shift Cycle-phase preservation
Structure Add one repetition Cycle-count flexibility
Structure Reorder two phrases Order preservation
Ornament Replace gamaka Ornament identity
Dynamic Change loudness Dynamic-relation preservation

G.3 Analysis

For each perturbation, record:

· Binary judgment (same / different).
· Reason (if given).
· Confidence (1–5).

Compute the invariant profile:

G_{\text{tradition}} = \{g : \text{practitioners consistently judge } g \cdot k \sim k\}

The complement is the discriminating set: perturbations that change the musical object.

G.4 Aggregation

For multiple practitioners, use:

· Majority rule for binary judgments.
· Median for ordinal judgments.
· Cohen's kappa to measure inter-rater agreement.

If agreement is low, the invariant is contested and should be reported as such, not resolved by fiat.

