Appendix H — Latency Use Case Details

H.1 The Problem

Earth–Mars round-trip latency is 6–44 minutes (depending on orbital positions). Real-time musical interaction across such distances is impossible under any event-based protocol.

H.2 Structural Solution

Instead of transmitting events, transmit structural descriptions:

\text{Node A sends: } \langle \text{cycle}, \text{period}, \text{phase}, t_0 \rangle

Node B reconstructs:

\text{events}(t) = \text{unroll}(\text{cycle}, \text{period}, \text{phase}, t)

for any t after t_0. Both nodes remain phase-locked locally, without further communication.

H.3 Why MIDI 2.0 Fails

MIDI 2.0's Universal MIDI Packet is a performance protocol. It transmits events in real time. Under 20-minute latency, every note arrives 20 minutes late. There is no native mechanism for "start a cycle at absolute time t_0 and continue indefinitely."

H.4 Why UMIR Succeeds

UMIR's G_{\text{cycle}} component represents cycles as first-class objects. A cycle is defined by period, phase, and content — not by a list of events. Shipping a cycle is shipping its definition.

H.5 Concrete Scenario

Setup. A musician on Earth and a musician on Mars want to play a shared rhythmic cycle.

Earth node.

· Sends: cycle(period=7, phase=0, content=..., t_0=2030-01-01T00:00:00Z).
· Waits 20 minutes.

Mars node.

· Receives cycle at t_0 + 20 min.
· Computes current phase: \phi = (t_{\text{now}} - t_0) \mod 7.
· Begins playing at correct phase, autonomously.

Result. Both nodes are phase-locked without continuous communication. They can adjust tempo by re-transmitting a new cycle definition (with a phase-continuity constraint), but the basic synchronization is achieved by structural representation, not event transmission.

H.6 Formalization

Let C = (p, \phi_0, \sigma) be a cycle. Let t_0 be the epoch. Then for any t \geq t_0:

\text{phase}(t) = (\phi_0 + t - t_0) \mod p

The reconstruction is deterministic and requires only C and t_0. Compare to event-based protocols, which require transmitting each event or pre-computing the entire horizon.

