Appendix I — Compiler Architecture Details

I.1 Compiler Interface

```
interface Compiler {
  target: TargetStandard
  compile: (K: KernelObject) → TargetObject
  preservationProfile: (K: KernelObject) → PreservationProfile
  canCompile: (K: KernelObject) → {yes, no, partial}
}

interface PreservationProfile {
  invariants: Map<InvariantId, PreservationValue>
  horizon: TimeHorizon
  collisions: List<Collision>
}
```

I.2 MIDI 2.0 Compiler

I.2.1 Event Mapping

For each e \in E:

· Onset t: encode as UMP timestamp.
· Pitch p: encode as note number (if discrete) or pitch-bend (if continuous).
· Duration d: encode as note-on / note-off interval.
· Amplitude a: encode as velocity and/or CC.
· Timbre \tau: encode as channel/voice assignment or Profile.
· Articulation \sigma: encode as CC.

I.2.2 Cycle Mapping

For each g \in G_{\text{cycle}}:

· Unroll the cycle over the horizon H.
· Encode the unrolled events as a sequence.
· Do not encode the cycle structure itself (not representable).

This is where collisions occur: the cycle structure is lost.

I.2.3 Constraint Handling

Universal constraints C:

· If expressible (e.g., "all pitches in a given scale"), encode as a scale constraint.
· If not, refuse compilation.

I.3 MusicXML Compiler

I.3.1 Event Mapping

· Time signature, divisions, measures.
· Notes with pitch, duration, voice, staff.
· Non-event fields become directions or articulations.

I.3.2 Cycle Mapping

For each g \in G_{\text{cycle}}:

· If the cycle period aligns with the measure length: encode as repeated measures.
· Otherwise: unroll as events, losing cycle structure.

I.3.3 Tuning

· Encode as <scordatura> or <tuning> if present.
· Otherwise: use <alter> for non-12-TET pitches (lossy).

I.4 MEI Compiler

I.4.1 Alignment Constraint

MEI requires that barlines align across staves. If the kernel has non-aligned cycles, the compiler must either:

· Refuse compilation, or
· Insert invisible barlines that approximate alignment (lossy).

I.4.2 Tuning

MEI supports four predefined temperaments. Non-standard tuning must be encoded as real numbers, introducing rounding.

