Appendix L — Failure Modes with Examples

L.1 FC1: No Collision Found

Statement. The thin slice finds no collision for any (kernel, target) pair.

Concrete scenario. Suppose K1 (cycle) compiles to a MIDI 2.0 stream that a conformant renderer treats differently from the unrolled version K_2. For example, suppose MIDI 2.0 has a native cycle construct (it does not, but for the sake of the scenario). Then T(K_1) \not\approx_{\text{MIDI}} T(K_2), no collision, no gap.

Implication. UMIR is redundant for that target. Revise or abandon.

L.2 FC2: No Kernel-Level Invariants

Statement. Phase 0 finds no invariants expressible in K without modules; module-level invariants are also absent.

Concrete scenario. Every tradition's invariants require a full grammar module, and no invariants survive across any two traditions without modules. The kernel is empty.

Implication. UMIR reduces to a module system. Reframe as a module composition framework.

L.3 FC3: Unmeasurable P_\iota

Statement. The graded preservation P_\iota cannot be computed reliably.

Concrete scenario. \varepsilon_r and \tau_\iota cannot be estimated with acceptable confidence intervals. Practitioners disagree on \tau_\iota by orders of magnitude.

Implication. The preservation profile cannot be claimed as a quantitative measure. Fall back to the binary collision test.

L.4 FC4: Practitioners Reject Collisions

Statement. Practitioners consistently reject the collisions identified in the thin slice as musically meaningless.

Concrete scenario. The cycle-vs-unrolled distinction is judged irrelevant by practitioners of the tradition under study: they consider a cycle and its unrolled realization to be the same musical object.

Implication. The invariant \iota_{\text{cycle}} is not admissible for that tradition. Remove or restrict.

L.5 FC5: Commutation Fails Trivially

Statement. The commutation test fails for all transformations because no target supports any transformation.

Concrete scenario. All target standards are restricted to fixed-tempo, fixed-tuning, event-based representation. No structural transformation commutes.

Implication. The commutation test reduces to "targets are structurally poor." This may be true but uninformative. Report as a limitation.

L.6 FC6: Kernel Algebra Trivial or Inconsistent

Statement. K is either trivial (all objects equivalent) or inconsistent (no well-formed objects exist).

Concrete scenario. The well-formedness conditions of Appendix C are mutually contradictory. For example, W4 (tree) and W5 (DAG) conflict if nodes are shared.

Implication. Fix the algebra or abandon the kernel.

