Appendix N — Open Problems

N.1 Formal

N.1.1. Is there a canonical choice of transformation group G for kernel-level invariants? Or is G inherently module-dependent?

N.1.2. Can the collision set \text{Col}(T, \iota) be characterized algebraically? For example, is it a subsheaf of \mathcal{K} \times \mathcal{K}?

N.1.3. Under what conditions does the graded preservation P_\iota converge as the horizon H \to \infty?

N.1.4. Is there a universal target standard X^* such that every UMIR invariant is preservable? If so, what is its structure? If not, what is the obstruction?

N.2 Computational

N.2.1. Can collision detection be automated? Given T and \iota, can a program enumerate \text{Col}(T, \iota) efficiently?

N.2.2. What is the complexity of compiling a kernel to a target with a prescribed preservation profile?

N.2.3. Can compilers be composed? Given T_1 : \mathcal{K} \to X_1 and T_2 : X_1 \to X_2, is T_2 \circ T_1 a valid compiler? What is its preservation profile?

N.3 Musical

N.3.1. Are there invariants that are preserved by all target standards? If so, are they trivial?

N.3.2. How does the choice of \varepsilon_r and \tau_\iota interact with perception? Are there universal perceptual limits?

N.3.3. Can the module system represent traditions whose grammar is not fully articulated? (Oral traditions, improvisation-heavy traditions.)

N.4 Civilizational

N.4.1. Is the latency use case the strongest motivation, or are there others?

N.4.2. How does UMIR interact with long-term archival? Are kernel objects more stable than standard-specific formats?

N.4.3. What is the failure mode of UMIR in a multiplanetary civilization? What happens if the kernel's invariants are not shared by non-human musicians?

